  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1217 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThe upper level low is currently hovering over northeastern \nND/northwestern MN/Canada border with a 1032mb high pressure \nsystem centered over Saskatchewan. Ongoing weak CAA and \nmarginal pressure rises (along with diurnal heating and a\nmarginally steep pressure gradient) winds will continue to be \ngusty until we lose the daytime heating, with gusts out of the \nnorthwest between 25-35 mph, highest over NESD/MN where the \noverlap in pressure rises/CAA/steeper pressure gradient lies, \ncloser to the low. As this system continues to exit the region \nand high pressure filters in, winds will diminish becoming calm \nto very light overnight. By 12Z Monday, the center of this large\n1034mb high will be right over the Dakotas where it will track \nsoutheast through the day/night, and strengthen, with the center\nof the ~1037mb high over IA by 12Z Tuesday and then tracking \neast.\n\nAs we head into Tuesday evening through Wednesday, a closed upper \nlow/trough will track southeastward out of Canada and forecast to be \ncentered over the region along with a weak west to east \noriented surface trough. However, with this strong high to our \neast ENS indicates an overall surface pressure of ~1026mb over \nthe CWA indicating lots of ongoing dry air at 925mb to the \nsurface per ENS/GEFS soundings. This is why models are \nstruggling and have been inconsistent on whether we will get any\nlight precip/QPF of 0.01\" or not over portions of NESD/MN. ENS \ncontinues to indicate the possibility of this (with the 06Z run \nindicating less overall coverage) with 00Z run meteograms at \nKABR/KATY/8D3 indicating the potential for light snow ranging \nfrom 25-40% chance, highest at KATY. GEFS indicates a lesser \nchance here and less areal coverage per drier air. Latest run of\nthe NBM has now added back in 15-30% pops Coteau and eastward \nwith a probability of QPF> 0.01\" 20-40% compared to 30-50% \nchance per mean GEFS/ENS/GEPS ensemble. So low confidence on \nwhether we get precip and if we do, higher confidence it would \nbe mainly Coteau and eastward at this point. As mentioned, any \nprecipitation that falls will be very light snow or flurries \nwith little to no accumulation expected and per warmer ground \ntemps. This leads to minimal travel impacts.\n\nColder air filtering in with this high (850mb temps between the\n10-25th percentile) along with little to no wind leads to a \nstrong radiational cooling setup overnight. The exception is \nNESD/MN as REFS indicates continuing broken to overcast lower \nclouds James Valley and eastward on the backside of the low and \ndiminishing in thickness and coverage a few hours before \nsunrise. Skies will overall clear across central SD with the \nexcept of some high clouds moving in over south central SD. Mean\nNBM lows range from around 10 to the upper teens. 25th \npercentile temps would run in the upper single digits to the mid\nteens across the CWA if we end up cooling further than \nforecasted. Probability of temps below 10 degrees is 30-45% \nover north central SD and highest over the Leola Hills and \nCorson County. So higher confidence on better radiational \ncooling leading to colder temps over north central and less \nconfidence on exact temps and strength of radiational cooling \nJames Valley and eastward. Highs for Monday will range in the \nupper 20s to the lower 30s which is about 5 degrees below normal\nfor this time of year. Beyond this, temps aloft to surface will\nwarm a bit into the 30s to lower 40s Tuesday and Wednesday, \nwhich is near normal.",
